# Novel MOF-Derived Co@N-C Bifunctional Catalysts for Highly Efficient Zn-Air Batteries and Water Splitting

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and MOF-derived materials have recently attracted considerable interest as alternatives to noble-metal electrocatalysts. Herein, the rational design and synthesis of a new class of Co@N-C materials (C-MOF-C2-T) from a pair of enantiotopic chiral 3D MOFs by pyrolysis at temperature T is reported. The newly developed C-MOF-C2-900 with a unique 3D hierarchical rodlike structure, consisting of homogeneously distributed cobalt nanoparticles encapsulated by partially graphitized N-doped carbon rings along the rod length, exhibits higher electrocatalytic activities for oxygen reduction and oxygen evolution reactions (ORR and OER) than that of commercial Pt/C and RuO2 , respectively. Primary Zn-air batteries based on C-MOF-900 for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) operated at a discharge potential of 1.30 V with a specific capacity of 741 mA h gZn-1 under 10 mA cm-2 . Rechargeable Zn-air batteries based on C-MOF-C2-900 as an ORR and OER bifunctional catalyst exhibit initial charge and discharge potentials at 1.81 and 1.28 V (2 mA cm-2 ), along with an excellent cycling stability with no increase in polarization even after 120 h - outperform their counterparts based on noble-metal-based air electrodes. The resultant rechargeable Zn-air batteries are used to efficiently power electrochemical water-splitting systems, demonstrating promising potential as integrated green energy systems for practical applications.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and MOF-derived materials have recently attracted considerable interest as alternatives to noble-metal electrocatalysts. Herein, the rational design and synthesis of a new class of Co@N-C materials (C-MOF-C2-T) from a pair of enantiotopic chiral 3D MOFs by pyrolysis at temperature Tis reported. The newly developed C-MOF-C2-900 with a unique 3D hierarchical rodlike structure, consisting of homogeneously distributed cobalt nanoparticles encapsulated by partially graphitized N-doped carbon rings along the rod length, exhibits higher electrocatalytic activities for oxygen reduction and oxygen evolution reactions (ORR and OER) than that of commercial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}$ and ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2},$ respectively. Primary Zn–air batteries based on C-MOF-900 for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) operated at a discharge potential of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1.30}\mathsf{v}}$ with a specific capacity of $747\ m\mathbf{A}\ h\ g_{z\mathsf{n}}{}^{-1}$ under $70\mathsf{m}\mathsf{A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . Rechargeable Zn–air batteries based on C-MOF-C2-900 as an ORR and OER bifunctional catalyst exhibit initial charge and discharge potentials at 1.81 and $\mathsf{1.28\vee(2\mathsf{m A}c m^{-2})}$ , along with an excellent cycling stability with no increase in polarization even after $\mathtt{l20h}$ – outperform their counterparts based on noble-metal-based air electrodes. The resultant rechargeable Zn–air batteries are used to efficiently power electrochemical water-splitting systems, demonstrating promising potential as integrated green energy systems for practical applications.
Oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) are the two most important reactions for electrochemical energy conversion and storage technologies with regard to fuel cells, metal–air batteries, and water electrolysis.[1–3] A key requirement for successful implementation of these electrochemical energy technologies is low-cost, highly active, and stable ORR and OER electrocatalysts.[4,5] Noble metals, like platinum (Pt) and ruthenium $(\mathrm{Ru})$ - or iridium (Ir)-oxides have been commonly used for ORR and OER, respectively.[6,7] However, their poor stability, high cost, and scarcity have precluded these noble metal-based oxygen electrocatalysts from large-scale applications.[4,5,8] Moreover, $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , or $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ are not suitable as bifunctional OER and ORR electrocatalysts. On the one hand, the in situ formed insulating platinum oxides deteriorate the OER activity of $\mathrm{Pt.}^{[8,9]}$ On the other hand, the ORR activity of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ is reduced by the in situ transformation to higher-valent oxides.[8,9] Therefore, the development of low-cost and durable bifunctional electrocatalysts with high performance for both
ORR and OER is a fundamental step toward the practical application of renewable electrochemical energy technologies.[2,10,11]
In recent years, nonprecious metals supported by N-doped carbon materials have become promising candidates as alternatives to noble metal-based catalysts for electrocatalysis.[12–14] In this context, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which contain metal moieties and organic linkers, are promising raw materials for the development of carbon-based electrocatalysts with atomically dispersed metal atoms as active sites.[15,16] Among them, ZIF-67 ( $\mathrm{\cdot\mathrm{ZIF}=}$ zeolitic imidazolate framework), consisting of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions and 2-methylimidazole, has been the most widely studied for ORR and OER in recent years.[17–20] Due to the short ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{Co}}$ distance with the 2-methylimidazole linkage in ZIF-67,[21] however, the metal atoms in ZIF-67 may aggregate easily into large particles with nonuniform distribution at high temperatures during pyrolysis that decreases the electrocatalytic activity.[19,21] To improve their electrocatalytic activity and stability, therefore, it is highly desirable to synthesize alternative MOF-based carbon materials of well-defined structures with homogeneously distributed active sites.
MIDPPA was synthesized by Suzuki cross-coupling and Ullmann condensation reactions (Figures S1–S5, Supporting Information).[22,23] Thereafter, solvothermal coordination reaction of MIDPPA, $1,2,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ , and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ afforded MOF-C2 with equal amounts of enantiotopic chiral 3D MOFs 1L and 1R, due to the same stabilities and solubilities of 1L and 1R during crystallization.[24] Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that 1L and 1R crystallized in the trigonal chiral space group R3.[25,26] Figure 1a presents the mirrored symmetrical coordination environments for 1L and 1R (cf. Figure S6 in the Supporting Information). For each $1,2,4{\cdot}\mathrm{btc}^{3-}$ anion in 1L or 1R, two carboxylate groups exhibit chelating–bridging bidentate connectivity, and another carboxylate group shows a syn-, anti-bridging bidentate mode (Figure S7a, Supporting Information). Ortho carboxylate groups of $1,2,4{\cdot}\mathrm{btc}^{3-}$ anions coordinate with metal atoms to form mirrored symmetrical
The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller specific surface area of C-MOF-C2-900, calculated to be $265\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}\ \mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ (Figure 2a), is higher than typical values for N-doped CNTs $(100~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[25] The Barrett–Joyner–Halenda pore size distribution curve (Figure 2b) derived from the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ desorption confirmed the presence of mesopores with diameters in the range of $2{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating a wide pore size distribution. Raman spectrum of C-MOF-C2-900 with an $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ value of 1.22 indicates the presence of abundant defects in the partially graphitized carbon (Figure 2c), which could act as potential active sites for electrocatalysis. The partial crystalline nature of C-MOF-C2-900 was further confirmed by the ring-like selected area electron diffraction (Figure S14a, Supporting Information) and powder X-ray diffraction (XRD, Figure 2d) with the broad XRD peaks at $26.2^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (002) diffraction of graphitic carbon, and another peak at $44^{\circ}$ attributable to the trapped Co nanoparticles. While the metallic Co nanoparticles can be observed by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and XRD, the $\mathrm{CoO}_{x}$ and $\mathrm{CoN}_{x}$ doped into the carbon network structure could be detected by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (vide infra).
The XPS survey spectrum of C-MOF-C2-900 (Figure S15a, Supporting Information) revealed the presence of carbon $(92.9\%)$ , cobalt $(1.2\%)$ , nitrogen $(0.7\%)$ , and oxygen $(5.2\%)$ species. The corresponding high-resolution XPS N 1s spectrum (Figure 2e and Table S3 (Supporting Information)) of C-MOFC2-900 was deconvoluted into pyridinic-N (398.6 eV, $55.2\%$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{N}}_{x}$ (399.4 eV, $26.9\%$ , and graphitic-N (401.0 eV, $17.9\%$ . The dominant ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ and pyridinic-N species play an important role as active sites in C-MOF-C2-900 for ORR and OER processes (vide infra).[30] The high-resolution Co $2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum (Figure 2f) of C-MOF-C2-900, along with the characteristic peaks associated with Co $(778.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ $(779.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ , and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ $(780.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ , indicates the presence of metallic cobalt and cobalt oxides with a synergistic effect on the electrocatalytic processes, as demonstrated previously.[30,31]
Deconvolution of the high-resolution XPS C 1s peak (Figure S15b, Supporting Information) of the C-MOF-C2-900 revealed the presence of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}\mathsf{C}$ (284.6 eV), $\mathrm{C-O/C-N}$ (285.6 eV), and ${\mathrm{C}}{=}0/{\mathrm{C}}{=}\mathrm{N}$ (291.7 eV). The high-resolution O 1s spectrum (Figure S15c, Supporting Information) can be fitted to three peaks at 530.2, 531.4, and $532.9\mathrm{eV},$ which correspond to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{O-Co}}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , and $\mathrm{C-O-H}$ , respectively. The thermogravimetric curve of C-MOF-C2-900 under air exhibited a sharp weight loss from 390 to $470^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $7.91\%$ ${\mathrm{Co}}_{x}{\mathrm{O}}_{\gamma}$ residue (Figure S16, Supporting Information).
InordertostudytheelectrocatalyticperformanceofC-MOF-C2, cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were initially carried out in a conventional three-electrode electrochemical cell in an $\mathrm{O}_{2^{-}}$ or $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ potassium hydroxide (KOH) solution. Furthermore, MOF-C2 was carbonized at 800 and $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain C-MOF-C2-800 and C-MOF-C2-1000 samples, in addition to C-MOF-C2-900, for investigating the influence of pyrolysis temperature on electrocatalytic performance. As shown in Figure 3a, the characteristic $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH oxygen reduction peak could be observed in the $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte at 0.69, 0.80, and 0.59 V (vs reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) for C-MOFC2-800, C-MOF-C2-900, and C-MOF-C2-1000, respectively, but not in the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ -saturated electrolyte. Among the three C-MOF samples, C-MOF-C2-900 had the best electrocatalytic activity with the highest positive peak potential. C-MOF-C2-900 also exhibited a half-wave potential $(E_{1/2}$ , $0.82\mathrm{V}$ in Figure 3b) better than C-MOF-C2-800 (0.66 V), C-MOF-C2-1000 (0.55 V) and even comparable to $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ .
To further understand the best performance of C-MOF-C2-900 among the C-MOF-C2-800, C-MOF-C2-900, and C-MOF-C2-1000 samples, electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS, Figure S17, Supporting Information) and XPS spectra (Figures S15, S18, and S19, Supporting Information) of these three samples were studied. As can be seen, the EIS results indicated a decreased resistance with increasing carbonization temperature from 800 to $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Generally speaking, the decrease in resistance could improve the performance of electrocatalysts.[32] Thus, C-MOF-C2-900 showed a superior ORR performance to C-MOF-C2-800. The better ORR performance of C-MOFC2-900 over the C-MOF-C2-1000 is attributed to the following aspects: (1) As indicated by the XPS full spectra (Figures S15a and S19a, Supporting Information), when the carbonization temperature increased from 900 to $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ the total nitrogen content decreased from $0.7\%$ to $0.3\%$ , and the total number of N-based active sites was diminished.[33] (2) Meanwhile, the more active ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{N}}_{x}$ $(26.9\%$ for C-MOF-C2-900) and pyridinicN $(55.2\%$ for C-MOF-C2-900) species were converted into less electrocatalytically active, but more thermally stable graphitic-N $(89.4\%$ for C-MOF-C2-1000, Figure S19b, Supporting Information).[5] (3) In addition, the cobalt particle size increased along with the aggregation of cobalt atoms, and hence the decreased catalytic activity for the C-MOF-C2-1000.[30,33,34] These combined factors led to poorer ORR performance for C-MOF-C2-1000 over C-MOF-C2-900.
Apart from the almost same half-wave potentials $(E_{1/2};0.817$ for C-MOF-C2-900 and $0.815\mathrm{~V~}$ for $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ in Figure 3b), C-MOFC2-900 showed a much better long-term stability than commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Figure S20a, Supporting Information) with less methanol crossover effect (Figure S20b, Supporting Information).
The ORR kinetics of C-MOF-C2-900 was also investigated according to the Koutecky–Levich (K–L) equation using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves measured at different rotational rates (Figure 3c).[35] The K–L plots (Figure S21, Supporting Information) of the C-MOF-C2-T catalysts over $0.5\mathrm{-}0.8\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 3c and Figure S21 (Supporting Information)) indicate $4\mathrm{e}^{-}$ reduction pathways based on the estimated numbers of transferred electrons per $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecule of 3.7–4.0, 3.8–4.0, 3.6–4.0 for C-MOF-C2-800, C-MOF-C2-900, and C-MOF-C2-1000, respectively.
Oxygen evolution activities were also studied to determine the suitability of MOF-C2-based carbon materials as bifunctional electrocatalysts for both ORR and OER. Among the three C-MOF-C2- $T$ samples obtained at 800, 900, and $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the C-MOF-C2-900 electrode achieved the highest OER catalytic activity in $0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ solution (Figure 3d). While the C-MOFC2-900 electrode showed the smallest onset potential (1.50 V) for all the electrodes tested (Figure 3d), it also outperformed the other electrodes at $10.0\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ with a potential of $1.58{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Figure 3d). Particularly, C-MOF-C2-900 (1.58 V) showed the best (lowest) potential at $10.0\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ $\left(E_{j=10}\right)$ than C-MOFC2-800 (1.65 V) and C-MOF-C2-1000 (1.73 V), even better than the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst (1.67 V). C-MOF-C2-800 (1.52 V) and C-MOFC2-1000 (1.55 V) exhibited comparable or even superior onset potentials to that of the $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst (1.52 V) and Pt/C (1.63 V).
The OER performance of ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ shown in Figure 3d is in good agreement with the reported standard results in terms of the onset potential $(1.50{-}1.60\mathrm{V})$ and $E_{j=10}$ (1.60–1.78 V),[2,14,36] and further supports the validity of our electrochemical measurements. Meanwhile, the Tafel slope values are 162, 79, 220, 99, and $230\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ for C-MOF-C2-800, C-MOF-C2-900, C-MOFC2-1000, $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , respectively (Figure 3e), which appear to follow the same trend as the OER reaction rate indicated by the LSV curves shown in Figure 3d. These results demonstrate that the OER activity of C-MOF-C2-900 is much better than all other samples tested, including $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ .
The observed excellent ORR and OER performance for C-MOF-C2-900 could be attributed to the following factors. First, the carbon rods with a hierarchical morphology are favorable for the transfer of reactants and electrons.[37] Second, the presence of cobalt–cobalt oxide/N-doped carbon hybrids and carbon-encapsulated Co nanoparticles, as demonstrated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), XPS, and TEM, can contribute greatly to the electrochemical ORR/OER.[36,38,39] Third, the high surface area of the carbon support facilitates the encapsulation of the metallic cobalt nanoparticles and the doping of Co into the carbon network structure, sustaining the stability during electrocatalysis.[38,40]
Durability evaluation by the i–t chronoamperometric response for OER showed that C-MOF-C2-900 and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ kept $93.1\%$ and $84.1\%$ retention of the initial OER activities after $50~000~\mathrm{s}$ , respectively (Figure 3f). SEM images of C-MOF-C2-900 after CV measurements ( $_{(0-1.8\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE for $30000\mathrm{~s~}$ , Figure S12e,f, Supporting Information) present no difference with those before CV test (Figure S12c,d, Supporting Information), demonstrating a superior stability of C-MOF-C2-900 for OER.
As discussed above, C-MOF-C2-900 contained multiple active sites, including metallic Co, $\mathrm{CoN}_{x}$ , $\mathrm{CoO}_{x},$ and $\mathrm{~N~}$ species, for OER and ORR. Previous studies have demonstrated that OER could be catalyzed by pyridinic-N, CoO, and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}[31,41]$ and ORR reactions could be catalyzed by CoO, $\mathrm{CoN}_{x},$ Co, and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},{}^{[41-43]}$ while carbon encapsulated metal nanoparticles were reported to show high catalytic activities.[44,45] However, it is difficult to claim one kind of active site for the observed high catalytic activities as some certain synergistic effects arising from different active sites cannot be ruled out.[41–43,46] In our case, it is the synergistic effect between the unique hierarchical rod-like structure, consisting of the homogeneously distributed cobalt nanoparticles encapsulated within the partially graphitized carbon ring phase along the rod length, and the ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{x}/{\mathrm{CoN}}_{x}$ -doped carbon network that enhanced the ORR/ OER performance of MOF-C2-900.
To demonstrate the application of C-MOF-C2-900 as a bifunctional catalyst within a $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air battery, we first constructed primary $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ –air batteries using C-MOF-C2-900 as the ORR electrocatalyst (Figure 4a and Figure S24 (Supporting Information)). Primary Zn–air batteries using C-MOF-C2-900 as the air electrode exhibited an open-circuit potential (OCP) as high as ${\approx}1.46\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 4b). As shown in Figure 4b, the C-MOF-C2-900 catalyst produced a higher maximum power density $(105\mathrm{~mw~cm^{-2}})$ than the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ in the identical primary Zn–air battery setup. The specific capacity of primary $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air batteries with C-MOF-C2-900 catalysts for ORR was tested at a current density of 5 and $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and compared to the theoretical capacity (Figure 4c). When normalized to the mass of consumed $Z\mathrm{n}$ , at a current density of 5 and $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , the specific capacity of the C-MOF-C2-900-based device was $768\mathrm{mAhg_{Zn}}^{-1}$ (at $5\operatorname{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ and $741\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g_{Zn}}{}^{-1}$ (at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2})$ ), which correspond to $93.7\%$ and $90.4\%$ , respectively, utilization of a calculated theoretical capacity ( $(820~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathrm{g_{Zn}}^{-1}$ ). Notably, the potential of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air battery using C-MOF-C2-900 (1.28 V) at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ is also higher than that of the battery using $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (1.20 V). Furthermore, no significant potential drop was observed during galvanostatic discharge for $40\mathrm{~h~}$ at 5 and $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 4d), indicating good catalytic stability of C-MOF-C2-900 for ORR in the practical metal–air battery. As shown in Figure 4e, the two Zn–air button batteries were connected in series to generate a sufficiently high OCP of ${\approx}2.8\ \mathrm{V},$ capable of powering a red light-emitting diode (LED, $5.0\times5.0$ mm, turn-on voltage: $1.7\mathrm{V},$ power: 0.06 W, brand: CREE). LEDs with four other colors were also powered by the two $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air button batteries based on C-MOF-C2-900, as shown in Figure S25 (Supporting Information).
In order to characterize and quantify the OER performance of C-MOF-C2-900, a rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ –air battery was set up in a similar configuration to the primary $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ –air battery (Figure 5a and Figure S26 (Supporting Information)), except for a different electrolyte $(6.0~\mathrm{~M~}~\mathrm{KOH}$ mixed with $0.2~\mathrm{~M~}~\mathrm{ZnOAc})$ . While C-MOF-C2-900 and commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalysts all exhibit similar charging and discharging potential at low current density $(<40\mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ),[2] C-MOF-C2-900 has a smaller potential difference (i.e., polarization) than the other catalysts at high current densities $(>40\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , Figure 5b). The smaller polarization indicates better performance at high current density for the C-MOF-C2-900-based Zn–air battery than the corresponding battery based on an air-cathode made from $\mathrm{Pt/C+}$ $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ mixture $(1:3,2.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ as a bifunctional catalyst for ORR and OER.[2]
A charge–discharge cycle test was performed with a current density of $2\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 5c and Figure S27 (Supporting Information)). As can be seen in Figure 5c, the initial charge and discharge potentials of C-MOF-C2-900 reached 1.81 and $1.28{\mathrm{V}},$ respectively, (1.83 and $1.27\mathrm{V}$ for $\mathrm{Pt/C}+\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ , Figure S27, Supporting Information), and after $120\mathrm{~h~}$ (360 cycles), the final values were 1.80 and $1.26\mathrm{~V~}$ for the device based on the C-MOF-C2-900 air-cathode (1.81 and $1.24\mathrm{~V~}$ for $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{RuO}_{2})$ . Under a higher current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure S28, Supporting Information), the initial charge and discharge potentials of C-MOF-C2-900 reached 2.00 and $1.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (2.00 and $1.20{\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{RuO}_{2})$ , and after $30\mathrm{~h~}$ (90 cycles), the final values were 2.00 and $1.16\mathrm{~V~}$ with increased polarization of
$0.02\mathrm{~V~}$ for the device based on the C-MOF-C2-900 air-cathode (2.00 and $1.14\mathrm{~V~}$ for $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ with an increased polarization of 0.06 V). Therefore, the C-MOF-C2-900-based rechargeable battery showed excellent performance and stability, outperforming its counterpart based on $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ air-cathode and most other reported bifunctional catalysts (Table S3, Supporting Information).
Two Zn–air batteries based on C-MOF-C2-900 were further integrated with a water-splitting unit based on the C-MOFC2-900 for OER and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ for HER. Figure 5d and Figure S29 (Supporting Information) display the two-electrode water-splitting device powered by two Zn–air batteries in series using C-MOF-C2-900 as the electrocatalyst. The water-splitting device uses carbon-fiber-woven cloths as electrodes $(2.5\times2.5~\mathrm{cm})$ 1 precoated with C-MOF-C2-900 (for OER) and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (for HER) (Figure S30, Supporting Information). $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ thus generated were collected and measured via water displacement (Figure 5e), and the Faradaic efficiency of the two-electrode setup was demonstrated to be close to $100\%$ for overall water splitting with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ volume ratio of 2.06:1, approaching the theoretical value of 2:1 during electrolysis (Figure 5e,f and Figure S31 (Supporting Information)). Due to the surface hydrophobicity of the carbon fiber electrode (e.g., $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ electrode), bubbles generated during the water-splitting process easily left the electrode surface, ensuring a good long-term stability. The gas production rates are as high as $10.1\upmu\mathrm{Ls}^{-1}$ for hydrogen gas and $4.9\upmu\mathrm{Ls}^{-1}$ for oxygen gas, showing great potential for practical applications.
The stability of C-MOF-C2-900 for water splitting was studied by chronopotentiometric testing. As can be seen in Figure S32 (Supporting Information), the overall water-splitting system, based on $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ for HER and C-MOF-C2-900 for OER, exhibited a potential of $1.61~\mathrm{V}$ to achieve $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at the initial stage, which quickly approached to a stable potential of ${\approx}1.68\ \mathrm{V},$ as observed in previous studies.[10] The observed stable potential for the water splitting showed no degradation even after $100~000~\mathrm{s}$ , indicating an excellent stability of C-MOFC2-900 for water splitting. Furthermore, the stable potential of ${\approx}1.68\mathrm{~V~}$ observed for our water-splitting system based on the $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (HER) and C-MOF-C2-900 (ORR) electrodes is lower than the corresponding values for many reported water-splitting systems using non-noble metal-based catalysts (Table S4, Supporting Information), including cobalt sulfide/carbon tube (1.74 V),[47] and NiFe layered double hydroxides (LDH)/nickel foam (NF) hydroxides (1.7 V).[48] Thus, C-MOF-C2-900 exhibited superb performance for water splitting.
In this study, $\operatorname{Co}\@limits{array}{r l} $ materials with hierarchical rodlike morphology were prepared via carbonization of a newly synthesized pair of enantiotopic chiral MOFs. The resultant C-MOF-C2-900 exhibited remarkable bifunctional electrocatalytic activity and stability toward both OER and ORR – outperformed commercial ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ electrocatalysts for OER and ORR, respectively. The observed excellent electrocatalytic activities can be attributed to the unique hierarchical rod-like structure of C-MOF-C2-900 with homogeneously distributed cobalt nanoparticles encapsulated by partially graphitized carbon rings along the rod length. Primary Zn–air batteries based on the C-MOF-C2-900 ORR air-cathode demonstrated a higher discharge potential of $1.30\mathrm{V}$ compared with those based on $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ air-cathode (1.20 V). The rechargeable Zn–air batteries based on C-MOF-C2-900 as the ORR and OER bifunctional aircathode exhibited excellent performance (initial charge and discharge potentials of 1.81 and $1.28{\mathrm{V}}$ under $2\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) and stability with almost no degeneration even after $120\mathrm{h}$ (300 cycles), outperforming their counterparts based on a $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}+\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ mixture $(1:3,2.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) as a bifunctional catalyst for ORR and OER. The C-MOF-C2-900-based rechargeable batteries were further used to power the water-splitting system based on the C-MOF-C2-900 for OER and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ for HER with a high stability and gas production rate at 10.1 and $4.9\upmu\mathrm{Ls}^{-1}$ for hydrogen and oxygen gas, respectively.
